WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
THE EFFECTIVENESS OF&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.520
CAPTCHA:
We’re all familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.940
this pesky pop up. Not only is it annoying, but&nbsp;
if we’re being honest, it’s kinda hard too. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:29.160
you just had to click a checkbox or type out&nbsp;
some blurry text. But, now you have to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.440
the motorcycle or the crosswalk or the invisible&nbsp;
donkey. Apparently these obtrusions are supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:40.920
protect websites from bots, hackers, and AI. But,&nbsp;
it looks like the only visitors that it’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:47.820
blocking are frustrated users. I mean, if AI is&nbsp;
advanced enough to produce this and this, surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:54.960
it can get through this and this right? Well, the&nbsp;
answer is yes, most modern AIs can easily bypass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.600
modern captchas. In fact, such AIs have become so&nbsp;
common that AI researchers have started to name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.140
their captcha solvers “Yet Another Text Captcha&nbsp;
Solver”. And that itself was 5 years ago. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.720
you don’t even need to know coding or develop AI&nbsp;
to outsmart these suckers. Instead, you can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.060
use cheap labor from overseas to crack millions&nbsp;
of these for virtually no money. In fact, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.860
are full on companies that are centered around&nbsp;
offering exactly this. The most notable of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:29.400
is a company called 2captcha who charges just $1&nbsp;
for 1000 captcha solves. You basically just need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.960
these ten lines of code here, and it’ll route&nbsp;
whatever captcha you’re trying to crack to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.480
sweatshop in Asia. And boom, you suddenly have the&nbsp;
ability to crack a million captchas for less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.400
the price of a new iPhone. Ironically, this is&nbsp;
not only more cost effective than hiring someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.280
to develop AI for you, but it’s actually even&nbsp;
more reliable. Afterall, the people solving these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:56.340
captchas are actually just real people. But, if&nbsp;
such solutions are so accessible and widespread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.560
why in the world are websites still making us&nbsp;
solve these increasingly difficult puzzles? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.500
it turns out that Captcha is not only an easy&nbsp;
revenue driver for Google, but it’s a goldmine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:14.400
of data and millions of hours of free labor.&nbsp;
Way back in 2007, 60 million captchas were being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.160
completed every single day, so this number is&nbsp;
likely in the hundreds of millions today most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.740
of which goes through Google. Google, ironically,&nbsp;
charges the same amount as the captcha solvers $1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.900
per 1000 solves, so with lets say 300 million&nbsp;
captchas per day, Google would be pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.360
in $110 million per year. Not a bad amount&nbsp;
for a service that doesn’t accomplish much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.940
but that money is nothing in comparison to the&nbsp;
data. Here’s the thing, what sort of website would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.160
be most likely to incorporate captcha? Well,&nbsp;
the answer is websites that offer some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.620
of valuable service whether that be a newsletter,&nbsp;
a waiting list, or an online purchase. This data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.940
point that you used this service is way more&nbsp;
valuable than the random Google search you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.920
made about how to be more popular. And thanks&nbsp;
to captcha, Google is able to get their hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.960
on a few hundred million of these data points&nbsp;
every single day. So, here’s how captcha went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.660
from being a failing security measure to being one&nbsp;
of Google’s most valuable data collection avenues.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.820
DUBIOUS HISTORY:
Interestingly enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:23.400
CAPTCHA’s weren’t invented to stop hackers. They&nbsp;
were actually invented by hackers to get around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.160
content screening and filtering. You see, back&nbsp;
in the 80s and 90s, computers couldn’t read text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.920
inside images very well, especially if they were&nbsp;
squiggly. So, obscure images with text became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.500
go to way for dubious people to communicate about&nbsp;
shady topics online without triggering any content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.780
filtering algorithms. Hackers would take this even&nbsp;
one step further by incorporating something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.760
leetspeak. You know how you can type “hello” on&nbsp;
a calculator using numbers? Well, it turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.040
that there’s actually a name for this, leetspeak,&nbsp;
and there’s an official way to use it. Computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.840
weren’t very good at interpreting leetspeak&nbsp;
either, so leetspeak plus squiggly images became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.940
the go to way to communicate about nefarious&nbsp;
topics online. Eventually though, at the turn of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.440
the century, this would catch the eyes of someone&nbsp;
from the other side: Carnegie Mellon University.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.940
A couple of researchers here figured that if&nbsp;
this strategy could be used to trick computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.100
for bad things, it could also be used to trick&nbsp;
computers for good things. One of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.380
issues online at the time was spambots and this&nbsp;
was way worse than we have it today. Nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:30.840
spambots are usually just annoying and tell you&nbsp;
to contact them through WhatsApp. But, back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.060
the day, spambots were spreading deadly viruses&nbsp;
like iloveyou, infecting tens of millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:41.460
computers and causing billions of dollars worth of&nbsp;
damage. So, naturally, these researchers wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.160
limit bots from accessing certain resources which&nbsp;
led to the creation of CAPTCHA in 1999. CAPTCHA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.140
stands for Completely Automated Public Turing&nbsp;
test to tell Computers and Humans Apart. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.980
it was basically just a piece of code that could&nbsp;
dynamically generate those seemingly harmless but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:02.280
infinitely frustrating tests. At first though, it&nbsp;
wasn’t too bad. In fact, it was actually easy for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.700
humans and hard for bots with humans boasting&nbsp;
a success rate of 97%. Seeing this, it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:13.560
take long for entrepreneurs to start implementing&nbsp;
this technology into their platforms, the first of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:19.020
which being Max Levchin. If you’re not familiar&nbsp;
with Max, he was one of the cofounders of PayPal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:25.200
and was serving as the company’s CTO. At the time,&nbsp;
PayPal was a prime target for hackers due to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.960
generous new account bonuses of $10 to $20. And&nbsp;
let’s just say the company was burning a boatload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.440
of money trying to keep up this promotion for&nbsp;
real users. This was the case until one night when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.840
a PayPal engineer named David Gausebeck would&nbsp;
have an epiphany. Why not use that hacker thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.120
to fight hackers? He would email his idea to Max&nbsp;
that night, and by the time he got to the office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.820
the next day, Max would already be half way done&nbsp;
with development. From there, it didn’t take long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:56.640
for PayPal to push out the feature in 2001, and it&nbsp;
quickly became an internet staple as everyone from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.280
eBay to Google adopted it. For years, it seemed&nbsp;
like this was the perfect solution to stop hackers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:08.220
from wasting resources and causing damage online,&nbsp;
but this golden period didn’t last forever.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:14.160
HACKERS STRIKE BACK:
With all these mega companies trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:20.040
to block hackers, you would think that they would&nbsp;
have no chance. But time and time again, this has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.900
proven to be false, and one of the main reasons&nbsp;
for this is that hackers simply have a far better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:30.120
risk to reward ratio. If they’re able to get&nbsp;
through the security of Google or Apple, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.220
the prospect of making millions if not tens of&nbsp;
millions. For Google and Apple on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:41.160
while such losses are somewhat annoying, they’re&nbsp;
by no means a top priority. It makes far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.260
sense for them to focus on protecting market share&nbsp;
and creating the next big thing as this is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:52.620
billions if not tens of billions. So, eventually,&nbsp;
hackers and researchers would both develop AIs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.900
and programs that could crack these relatively&nbsp;
simple questions. It was clear that CAPTHAs needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:02.220
to be taken to the next level, but it didn’t&nbsp;
make sense for each company to develop these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:07.800
increasingly difficult CAPTCHAs by themselves. It&nbsp;
was simply too much effort. What they needed was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.260
a centralized service with a dedicated team that&nbsp;
could always stay one step ahead of hackers. Enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:18.600
Google. In September of 2009, Google would acquire&nbsp;
reCAPTCHA from the original Carnegie Mellon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:23.520
researchers for an undisclosed amount. Honestly,&nbsp;
it was probably a really small amount for Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.740
maybe $5 or $10 million. But, given that the&nbsp;
researchers were having trouble trademarking their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.120
findings, their only alternative to making more&nbsp;
money was to build a full on company around this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.680
so they would agree to the deal. Google, on the&nbsp;
other hand, would turnaround and 200 IQ this. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.160
they would go ahead and use it for data&nbsp;
collection but we’ll get to that later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:47.280
They actually had a 200 IQ move way before that.&nbsp;
Why generate useless pieces of text for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:53.280
to transcribe when you can get them to transcribe&nbsp;
useful pieces of text? You see, around this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.500
Google was trying to digitize the entirety of the&nbsp;
world’s newspapers and books for Google Books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:03.960
But, it turns out that it was quite difficult for&nbsp;
computers to optically recognize text from older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:09.060
books and newspapers. So, Google decided that&nbsp;
it would be a great idea to incorporate these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:14.640
hard to read words into CAPTCHA tests. For&nbsp;
one, if Google’s AIs couldn’t decipher them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.380
it was unlikely that malicious AIs could. And two,&nbsp;
Google could literally get paid by websites and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:25.080
companies to transcribe these old books using&nbsp;
free labor, and lots of it. Back in the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:30.480
people were solving 60 million captchas every&nbsp;
single day, and even if we assume that it only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.960
takes 5 seconds on average to complete, we’re&nbsp;
talking about over 83,000 hours of labor. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.680
Google wanted to hire this same amount of&nbsp;
man power, they would have to hire over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:48.180
10,000 employees. Even at minimum wage, that&nbsp;
would cost them $145 million per year, but using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:54.540
this strategy, Google was getting paid to do this,&nbsp;
and this takes us into the era of Google CAPTCHA.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.040
CAPTCHA BECOMES USELESS:
Google’s clever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:05.220
book idea gave them free labor for years, but&nbsp;
eventually their AIs did become advanced enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.360
to decode any piece of text and so did malicious&nbsp;
AIs leading us to our modern conundrum. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.580
Google would try to address this issue in the&nbsp;
most obvious way possible, just make the CAPTCHA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:21.780
harder. But, by 2014, it became clear that this&nbsp;
wasn’t gonna work. CAPTCHAs had already become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.880
the opposite of what they were supposed to be.&nbsp;
Instead of being easy for humans and hard for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:31.200
bots, they were hard for humans and a joke for&nbsp;
bots. I’m not even kidding. Bots were passing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:38.580
text CAPTCHAs 99.8% of the time while humans&nbsp;
were only succeeding 33% of the time. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:43.080
text CAPTCHAs had reached the end of their&nbsp;
useful lives, prompting Google to create the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:48.420
famous I’m not a robot checkbox. The idea of&nbsp;
this new CAPTCHA was that instead of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:53.880
test the intelligence of humans, it would test the&nbsp;
imperfectness of humans. I know a lot of us like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:59.100
to think that we’re mouse aficionados with our&nbsp;
fancy gaming mice, but it turns out that humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:04.800
actually suck at using mice. If you zoom into our&nbsp;
mouse movement, there’s actually a lot of quivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:10.080
shakes, and general randomness. A bot on the other&nbsp;
hand is able to click on the checkbox with precise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.980
mouse movement or even no mouse movement at&nbsp;
all. It can just call the backend code that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:16.860
takes place when a mouse click happens, but this&nbsp;
would be a mistake because Google is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:22.740
looking for our imperfectness. This was not only&nbsp;
a brilliant solution but it was easy for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.660
to use. I mean, you just click on the box, but&nbsp;
there was one fatal flaw. This system doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:30.000
really work on mobile devices because there is&nbsp;
no mouse. So, Google turned to creating a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.880
version of the original text CAPTCHA. Bots had&nbsp;
become really good at deciphering text, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.980
couldn’t yet decipher images like crosswalks and&nbsp;
invisible donkeys. And this led to the creation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:46.020
of the modern CAPTCHA that we all hate today.&nbsp;
Things didn’t get better from there either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:51.540
It didn’t take long for bots to be able to mimic&nbsp;
the imperfect mouse movement of humans, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.700
led to the picture CAPTCHA being ported over to&nbsp;
computers as well. But, as you would guess, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.860
too was quickly solved. The most ironic part about&nbsp;
this is that many of the so called hacks actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:03.240
use Google’s own image recognition technology&nbsp;
to crack the CAPTCHA. So, more recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.280
Google has opted to completely ditch tests&nbsp;
altogether with reCAPTCHA v3. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.300
the CAPTCHA is invisible and is constantly&nbsp;
tracking your interaction with a given website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:17.580
to assign you a dynamic risk score between&nbsp;
0 and 1. Website owners can set a threshold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:23.400
under which users will be denied access to the&nbsp;
given resource. But, it’s not really clear how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:24.600
effective this really is, and judging by the&nbsp;
history of CAPTCHA, it’s only a matter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.640
time until this too is circumvented if it&nbsp;
hasn’t already. So, CAPTCHA’s have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:32.820
virtually useless, but if that’s the case, why&nbsp;
in the world does everyone still use CAPTCHA?

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:38.580
THE STATE OF CAPTCHA:
CAPTCHAs are no longer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:44.040
great at stopping bots, but that isn’t to say&nbsp;
that they have no value for anyone. For Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:49.140
CAPTCHA offers tremendous value. Before they were&nbsp;
able to train their text recognition technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:54.060
and digitize books using CAPTCHA. Now, they’re&nbsp;
able to collect millions of hours worth of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.860
image recognition and general website usage data&nbsp;
that can be used for machine learning and training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:04.620
AI. And it doesn’t stop right there either,&nbsp;
Google is also able to collect data about our most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:10.080
important online activity. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;
our most important online activity is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:16.140
guarded by CAPTCHAs giving Google direct access to&nbsp;
this specific data which is way more valuable than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:22.200
our general online usage. So, it’s no wonder why&nbsp;
Google continues to push CAPTCHA and put resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:29.400
behind it, but what about companies and websites.&nbsp;
Why use something that’s not even effective? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:36.540
the primary reason is why not? You see, as CAPTCHA&nbsp;
became less and less effective, Google became more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:41.280
and more generous with their free tiers for&nbsp;
companies. Currently, Google offers the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:47.040
million calls to CAPTCHA every month for free.&nbsp;
And most websites aren’t protecting access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:52.800
the website itself with CAPTCHA. Usually, CAPTCHA&nbsp;
is used to protect new account creation, joining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:58.620
a waiting list, buying a newsletter, etc. And&nbsp;
even the best websites in the world only have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:04.020
conversion rate of 11% meaning that you would need&nbsp;
to have nearly 10 million visitors to your website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:10.860
every month before Google even charges you a dime.&nbsp;
And even after that, they’re only gonna charge you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:17.640
around a $1000 for every 10 million visitors. So,&nbsp;
it’s really only the mega billion dollar companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:23.100
that are paying Google for CAPTCHA, and it’s&nbsp;
a pretty negligible cost for them as well. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:31.200
why not use it? Sure, it may let 90 or even 99% of&nbsp;
bots pass right on through, but it costs next to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:36.540
nothing or literally nothing and it may stop&nbsp;
a few bots on occasion and give it a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:42.060
extra security. Not to mention, everyone else is&nbsp;
using it, so using it will make your website look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:47.100
more reputable. And that’s why everyone still&nbsp;
uses CAPTCHA despite it being horrendously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:53.340
ineffective and annoying. Do you hate CAPTCHAs?&nbsp;
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:57.900
suck at CAPTCHAs too. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:01.500
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

